module Darklang.Cli.Docs.Traits

let content () : String =
  """# Traits

A trait names one or more fns over one open type; an implementation says how one type
does them; a call picks by the type of the value it is given. Nearly every operator is a
trait (`+` is `Add`, `<` is `Compare`; `==` is not), as is `toString`, so every
builtin type already has implementations. Yours join with one `impl`.

## Implement a trait that exists
  type Point = { x: Int64; y: Int64 }

  impl ToString for Point =
    let toString (p: Point) : String =
      $"({Stdlib.toString p.x}, {Stdlib.toString p.y})"

  impl Add for Point =
    let add (a: Point) (b: Point) : Point =
      Point { x = a.x + b.x; y = a.y + b.y }

  let p = Point { x = 1L; y = 2L }
  ToString.toString p          // "(1, 2)"
  p.toString                   // the same call, receiver first
  p + p                        // Point { x = 2L; y = 4L }

  A member can name a fn that already exists instead of declaring one:
    impl ToString for Point =
      let toString = Acme.pointToText

## Declare your own
  trait Area<'a> =
    let area (shape: 'a) : Float

  impl Area for Circle =
    let area (c: Circle) : Float = 3.14159 * c.r * c.r

  impl<'a: Area> Area for List<'a> =     // conditional: needs Area for the element
    let area (xs: List<'a>) : Float =
      Stdlib.sum 0.0 (Stdlib.List.map xs (fun s -> Area.area s))

  The first type parameter is the type an implementation is for. A method can
  cap what an implementation may do (`let fetch (u: 'a) :{Http} String` is Http and
  nothing else); `docs permissions` has the effect names.

## Bound a type parameter
  let describe<'a: ToString> (v: 'a) : String = "value: " + v.toString
  let biggest<'a: Compare + ToString> (xs: List<'a>) : String = ...

  `'a: ToString` means "any type with a ToString implementation". It is checked
  when the fn is entered, against the caller's actual type, and statically by
  the checker (`MissingImpl`, `UnboundTypeParameter`).

## Where they live
  A trait is a package item like a type or a fn: `Stdlib.ToString`. An
  implementation is one too, named for the type and the trait under the
  module it was written in: `impl ToString for Point` inside `Acme` is
  `Acme.Point.ToString`, and its method fn is `Acme.Point.ToString.toString`.

  So an implementation's methods live in a module named for the trait. A listing
  shows you the implementation rather than that module, since they are the same
  thing under two headings: `ls`, `tree` and `view` all agree. `view` on the
  implementation prints the whole block; `ls` on it lists its methods, and `view`
  on one of those reads it on its own.

    dark ls Acme                        // Traits and Impls sections
    dark view Stdlib.ToString           // prints the trait back
    dark view Acme.Point.ToString       // the implementation
    dark ls Acme.Point.ToString         // its methods
    dark view Acme.Point.ToString.toString
    dark impls ToString                 // every implementation a call can reach
    dark search Area --trait            // --impl for implementations

  Author from the shell like `fn` and `type`; an impl's path is the module it
  is written in:
    dark trait Acme.Area "<'a> = let area (shape: 'a) : Float"
    dark impl Acme "Area for Circle = let area (c: Circle) : Float = ..."

  A bare trait name (`Area`, `Add`) resolves from your module outward, then as
  `Stdlib.<name>`; write the full path when two traits share a name.

## The stdlib traits
  Add Subtract Multiply Divide Modulo Power Negate    the operators `+ - * / % **` and `-x`
  BitwiseAnd BitwiseOr BitwiseXor BitwiseNot     `& | ^` and `~x`
  ShiftLeft ShiftRight                        `<<` and `>>`
  Compare                            `< <= > >=`; also `Stdlib.max`, `min`, `clamp`
  ToString                       `x.toString`, `Stdlib.toString x`
  Zero, One                      `Zero.zero<Int64> ()`; `Stdlib.sum seed xs` and
                                 `product seed xs` take a seed, so summing an
                                 empty list has an answer

  Every builtin numeric type implements the arithmetic, comparison and bitwise
  operators; String implements Add (`"a" + "b"`) and Compare, so `"a" < "b"` works;
  Char and DateTime implement Compare too.

  Two things worth knowing. Your own `impl Add for Int64` is stored, and a call on
  two operands of one builtin numeric type does not reach it: those are added
  directly. And `==` is not a trait: equality is structural for every value and no
  type can say otherwise.
  `docs operators` has the operator table.

## Which implementation a call runs
  It is decided when you SAVE, not when the call runs. The checker knows the type
  the call is on, so the implementation is chosen then and stored with the call:
  the code goes on doing what it did when you wrote it, and an implementation
  written later does not change it underneath you. It reaches you the way any
  other update does, through `dark propagate` (and `pin` refuses it), because a
  stored call depends on that implementation's fn like any other call.

  Operators are stored the same way, inside a pipeline too. A call in a bounded fn
  (`let display<'a: ToString> (v: 'a) = v.toString`) stores "whatever my caller's 'a
  implied", and the caller stores which implementation that was, so it is not a
  run-time lookup either. What is left for run time: a call whose type nothing could
  work out when it was saved.

## Two implementations for one type
  A call finds an implementation anywhere on the branch, whatever module it was
  written in: modules do not open, and where an implementation lives is only its
  name. Two implementations for one trait and type is allowed, and the call runs
  the one written later, by the same rule that settles two people writing to one
  name, so every machine runs the same one. The pair shows up as a
  `rival-implementations` finding in `dark constraints`: ack it, or deprecate one
  to settle it, since no new call picks a deprecated implementation. A call SAVED
  against one before it was deprecated goes on running it, which is the same rule
  that makes a saved call stable against a newer rival. `dark impls <Trait>` marks
  the one that is not used. The only case that errors is two
  implementations a script created in memory, where nothing says which came
  later.

  Deprecating the LAST one is a different thing, and it is loud: nothing can
  discharge the trait for that type any more, so saving a call that needs it
  fails the at-rest check with `MissingImpl` rather than waiting for the call
  to run. Existing saved calls keep the implementation they resolved against,
  which is what makes retiring one safe to do before the callers are moved.

  Two traits with the same methods and shapes are one trait, whatever they are
  named.

## Errors, as they read
  Bool has no implementation of Darklang.Stdlib.Add. Write `impl Add for Bool =
  ...`, or run `dark impls Add` to see which types do

  2 implementations of Darklang.Stdlib.ToString for Int64 exist, and there is no
  record of which came first, so none is used
      only for implementations a script made in memory

  Zero.zero has no argument to read a type from, so it cannot tell which type
  you mean. Name it: `Zero.zero<Int64> ...`

  Cannot perform numeric operation on Int64 and Float
      both operands of an operator must be the same type"""
